@node INTRODUCTION "Introduction"

    The original idea with D(X)Make was to provide a powerful make utility
    through general features rather than specialized hacks.  It is governed
    by a few simple rules that can be combined to produce complex and
    powerful operations.

    SDMake was made of of the need for a more versatile make utility than
    SMake. And one that was not the first step on the way of turning my
    dev-environment into POSIX. So honestly it was mostly about the fact
    that GNU make is NIH, or rather NIOA - Not Invented On Amiga. And also
    it is too big.

@endnode
@node CLIUSAGE "Shell Usage"

  When starting from shell the following options are supported:

    SDMake [-f file] [-n] [-D variable[=value]] [-d[N]] [-F[N]] [-i] [-a]
            [-c[N]] [-q] [-s] [-S] [-t] [-h] [targets...]

    -f file

        Selects the SDMakefile to open.
        If this argument is not supplied SDMake will try to open
        SDMakefile. If that fail it will try DMakefile, SMakefile and
        Makefile in that order.

    -n

        Dry run, do no run any commands.

    -D variable[=value]

        Define a variable, and optionally set it to value.

    -d[N]

        Set the debug level. If no number is specified, a debug level
        of 1 is used.
        (This only works if compiled with "define=USE_DEBUG=1")

    -F[N]

        Set the default failat level for command lists. If no number
        is specified, a level of 6 is used.

    -i

        Ignore error returns from commands. This is equivalent of putting
        '-' in front of every command in all command lists.

    -a

        Force all dependencies to be run as if they were out of date.
    
    -c[N]

        Set the cache level:
          0 = No cache. Examine() is called every time a file is
              encountered. Even if it was examined earlier in the
              same run.
          1 = Cache a files timestamp the first time it is
              encountered, and use the cached value if the same file
              appears again. This is the default setting.

    -q

        Determines if the selected target(s) are current.  SDmake
        prints a 1 (and return OK) if all the selected target(s)
        are current or a 0 (and return WARN) if not. Can be
        combined with -S to disable the output completely, and
        contrary to SMake it will still return WARN it any selected
        target(s) are not current.

    -s

        Do not echo command to the screen before executing them.

    -S

        Generate less verbose output.

    -t

        Touches the target files by updating them with the current
        system time. SDMake does not execute any of the actions
        associated with these targets.

    -h

        Summarize the version and available command line options.

    targets...

        One or more target(s) to run. If none are supplied SDMake
        will run the first target it finds in the SDMakefile.

@endnode
@node WBUSAGE "Workbench Usage"

  When started from Workbench, SDMake reads the tool types from its own icon,
  then from any icons that were Shift-selected, and finally from the icon
  that was actually double-clicked.

  If a tool type that is not marked with "Can appear multiple times..."
  appears multiple times, it will be set to the value of the last
  occurrence of that tool type.

  The following tool types are supported:

    FILE

        Selects the SDMakefile to open.
        If this argument is not supplied, SDMake first tries to
        open the file associated with the icon that was double-clicked.
        If the icon has no associated file, it falls back to trying
        SDMakefile, DMakefile, SMakefile, and Makefile, in that order, in the
        directory of the double-clicked icon.

    DRYRUN

        If set to any value > 0, do a dry run, do no run any commands.

    TARGET

        Select a target to run.
        Can appear multiple times, to select multiple targets.
        If not present the first target of the selected SDMakefile
        will run.

    DEFINE

        Define a variable, and optionally set it to value.
        Can appear multiple times, to define multiple variables.

    FAILAT

        Set the default failat level for command lists. If this tool
        type is not present the default level of 6 is used.

    IGNORE

        If set to any value > 0, ignore error returns from commands.
        This is equivalent of putting '-' in front of every command
        in all command lists.

    DOALL

        If set to any value > 0, force all dependencies to be run as
        if they were out of date

    CACHE

        Set the cache level:
          0 = No cache. Examine() is called every time a file is
              encountered. Even if it was examined earlier in the
              same run.
          1 = Cache a files timestamp the first time it is
              encountered, and use the cached value if the same file
              appears again. This is the default setting.

    SILENT

        If set to any value > 0, do not echo command to the screen
        before executing them.

    QUIET

        If set to any value > 0, generate less verbose output.

    DEBUG

       Set the debug level.
       (This only works if compiled with "define=USE_DEBUG=1")

    CONSOLE

       Define the console window to use.
       The default is equivalent to:
       CONSOLE=con:10/10/400/150/SDMake/CLOSE

@endnode
@node SDMAKEFILE "SDMakefile"

    The SDMakefile may contain four different kinds of lines.

    Any line may be continued by terminating it with a backslash '\'.

    It is possible to escape the special characters '$' and '%' by doubling
    them though this is only necessary if an open-parenthesis follows the
    '$' or '%' and you do not want it interpreted as a variable.

    It is possible to escape ':' and other special characters by assigning
    them (or a string containing them) to a variable and then using the
    variable.

    The four kinds of lines are:

    (1) COMMENTS -- Any line beginning with a '#' is a comment and ignored

    # This SDMakefile generates an executable for fubar, the compiler
    # options are as follows ...


    (2) ASSIGNMENTS -- Any line of the form <SYMBOL> = ...  is considered
	an assignment.	Any variable references from within the assignment will
	be resolved immediately.

	There are a number of variations on assignments.

	SYMBOL= data		Normal assignment, overwrite any previous
				assignment.

	SYMBOL ?= data		Conditional assignment only if SYMBOL has
				not already been defined.

	Examples:

	    CFLAGS= -r
	    SRCS= x.c y.c z.c

	Variables are typically referenced using $(SYMBOL) but there are
	other @{"combinations" link "WILDCARDS"}, including search-and-replace combos, which
	are often used to shortcut mass dependencies like 'all these
	object files depends on all these source files', which are
	described later.


    (3) DEPENDENCIES: -- A line starting with one or more symbols followed
    by a colon (or two), with a whitespace on either or both side of the
    colon(s), is assumed to be a dependency. Dependencies list one or more
    destination-files/targets on the left-hand-side (lhs), and zero or more
    source-files/prerequisites on the right-hand-side (rhs).

    Absolute paths are supported, but you cannot have a raw path that
    begins with ':', or begin a dependency line with a raw filename that
    begins with '.', as this confuses SDMake. For weird filenames the
    solution is to use a variable to hold the filename and then specify
    $(VARIABLE) in the dependency.

    Following the dependency line is zero or more command lines --
    commands to run to resolve the dependency, terminated with a
    blank line. Command lines must begin with a tab or a space.

    NOTE: Dependencies do not have to have commands associated with them,
    and you can also specify a target several times.  So, for example,
    you can have a 'a.o : defs.h' target that is empty and a
    'a.o : a.c' target which compiles the source file into an object
    file.  If any dependency is triggered, all commands associated
    with that dependency whether they are specified in that particular
    section or not will be executed.

    dst1 ... dstN : src1 ... srcN
	command1
	command2  ...

    dst1 ... dstN : src1 ... srcN
	command1
	command2  ...

    Finally, note that a dst* or src* symbol does not need to be a
    filename.  It is perfectly valid to make up dummy names which are then
    used as the lhs of a dependency whos purpose is to collect other
    dependencies together.


    (4) DIRECTIVES: -- A line beginning with a '.' is assumed to be a
    @{"directive" link "DIRECTIVES"}.

@endnode
@node DEPENDENCIES "Dependencies"

			      DEPENDENCIES

    When declaring dependencies you may use four different forms.  The
    first form is to have a single destination and several sources.  This
    is interpreted to mean 'execute the command list to convert ALL the
    specified sources to the specified target'.  The special variable,
    %(left), is set to the target symbol and the special variable %(right)
    is set to ALL of the source symbols.

    For example, this form would be used to indicate that an executable
    depends on all the objects being resolved before you can run the link.

	(1) dst : src1 src2 src3 ... srcN

    The second form is the most useful in that it allows you to specify
    multiple 1 : 1 dependencies.  Thus, you can specify, for example, that
    each object file depends on its source file counterpart for ALL the
    files in your project on a single line and have a single command list
    representing what to do (to compile a source file into an object, say).

    In this case %(left) and %(right) are set to each dst* : src* pair
    individually and the command list is run for any individual pair that
    is out of date.

	(2) dst1 dst2 dst3 ... dstN : src1 src2 src3 ... srcN

    The next form may be used to specify that many target files depend
    on one source file.  The command list will be run once with all
    the targets specified in %(left) and the single source specified
    in %(right).

	(3) dst1 dst2 dst3 ... dstN : src

    The last form is esoteric but sometimes useful.  EACH target on the left
    hand side depends on the entire right hand side.  You can have an
    arbitrary number of symbols on either side.  %(left) will be set to a
    particular target while %(right) will be set to all of the sources.

    for example, you could specify $(OBJS) :: $(HDRS)  -- make all objects
    depend on all headers causing a recompile to occur if any header is
    modified (ok, this isn't the best example but you get the idea).

	(4) dst1 dst2 dst3 ... dstN :: src1 src2 ... srcI

    This is also the form that must be used if you have several destinations
    and zero sources, else you will get a error.

@endnode
@node ADVDEPENDENCIES "Advanced Dependencies"

			ADVANCED DEPENDENCIES

    SDMake implements a number of special cases for dependencies.

    SPECIAL CASE #1 - DIRECTORY CREATION / FILE EXISTANCE

	A target can depend on itself.  This is typically used to create
	a directory.

	obj : obj
	    mkakedir obj

    SPECIAL CASE #2 - NO SOURCE SPECIFIED

	A target can be supplied without any source dependencies.  Such
	targets are executed unconditionally and if they are included as
	a source to another target's dependency that other target will
	also be executed unconditionally.

	all:	target
	    echo "This will also run unconditionally"

	target:
	    echo "This will run unconditionally"

    SPECIAL CASE #3 - COMMANDS EXECUTE BUT TARGET NOT UPDATED

	This case is differentiated from Case #2 in that it occurs when
	the target exists but is not updated by the commands that are
	executed.  SDMake will automatically touch the target when it
	detects this case and then it will treat the target as having
	been up-to-date, so items depending on the target do not execute
	their own command lists.  Those items, if they are real files,
	will also be touched.  This case is used to better handle
	generated files which wind up not being any different then what
	was present before.

	protos.h : $(SRCS)
	    grep '^Prototype' %(right) > %(left).new
	    cmp %(left) %(left).new || mv -f %(left).new %(left)

    SPECIAL CASE #4 - LEAF TARGETS

	The dependency chain terminates either at an unconditional target
	(where no source is specified), or at an unspecified target.  A
	good example of this is when you have an object file depending on
	a source file and you do not specify the source file as a target
	dependent on something else.

	In this case SDMake compares the timestamp of the source file(s)
	against the target and executes the command list if the target
	is out of date or does not exist.  In the below example 'fubar.c'
	is a leaf target used as a source which fubar.o depends upon.

	fubar.o : fubar.c
	    sc fubar.c

@endnode
@node WILDCARDS "Wildcards"

			WILDCARDED FILE LIST REPLACEMENT
				VARIABLE ACCESS

    SDMake's most powerful feature is an easy to use file list replacement
    through options in a variable specification.  You may insert the
    contents of any variable using the form:

	$(SYMBOL)

    Furthermore, you can make modifications to the contents of the variable
    on the fly using:

	$(SYMBOL:wildcard)              only those files which match <wildcard>
	$(SYMBOL:wildcard:wildcard)     matching files and also do a conversion

    Or

	$(SYMBOL:"wildcard")
	$(SYMBOL:"wildcard":"wildcard")

    Simple */? wildcarding is available.  A wildcard may contain a colon or
    other punctuation but if it does you MUST surround it with quotes.  The
    data represented by each '*' or '?' on the left hand side may be
    inserted on the right hand side using %1, %2, %3, etc.  You can also
    use "*" and "?" on the right hand side (both are interpreted the same way)
    as an iterator for %1, %2, etc.  Here is a quick example:

	SRCS= a.c b.c c.c d.c xx.a
	OBJS= $(SRCS:*.c:"/tmp/%1.o")
	# another way of saying the same thing:
	OBJS= $(SRCS:*.c:"/tmp/*.o")

	all:
	    echo $(OBJS)

	(the result is '/tmp/a.o /tmp/b.o /tmp/c.o /tmp/d.o')

    The first wildcard specification restricts which files from the list
    are to be taken -- 'xx.a' was ignored, as you can see.  Each '*' or '?'
    in the first wildcard specification corresponds to %N specifications
    or "*" or "?" specifications in the second wildcard.  You can prepend,
    delete, insert, or append text and freely mix or ignore items matched
    to create your destination file list.

    This capability allows you to specify your source files EXACTLY ONCE
    in the SDMakefile and then use the file munging capability to convert
    them to the object file list, etc...

    You can embed variables within variables as with the following example
    (note that this time xx.a is included):

	OD= obj/
	SRCS= a.c b.c c.c d.c xx.a
	OBJS= $(SRCS:*.?:"$(OD)%1.o")

	all:
	    echo $(OBJS)


	(the result is: 'obj/a.o obj/b.o obj/c.o obj/d.o obj/xx.o')

    As a side note, you may also specify '?' and '*' in the destination
    wildcard.  These are considered dummies and are equivalent to %N where
    N is incremented from 1..9 for each '?' or '*' encountered.

    You can use this capability anywhere in the SDMakefile.  Another common
    thing to do is restrict your link line to include only the object files
    and skip the headers:

	$(EXE) : $(PROTOS) $(OBJS) $(HDRS)
	    sc link to %(left) %(right:*.o)

    Additionally, in command lists, variables can also be selected using
    wildcards. This requires quoting the variable in single-quotes like the
    following example:

        oneOBJ = one.o
        twoOBJ = two.o

        all: one two

        one two ::
              echo %(left:*:"obj/'$(*OBJ)'")

        (this results in the commands "echo obj/one.o" and "echo obj/two.o")


@endnode
@node SPECIALVARIABLES "Special variables"

			   SDMAKE SPECIAL VARIABLES

    SDmake has four special variables that gets created automatically.

    The first one to make it easier for a SDMakefile to know where in the
    directory hierarchy it stands relative to the current directory (where
    the original SDMake was run).  $(TOPDIR) is empty relative to the
    SDMakefile in the current directory where the SDMake program is run.
    If a SDMakefile has an ".include" directive the directory prefix
    specified in that directive becomes the new $(TOPDIR) for processing of
    that include file.  Nested .include directives (if not absolute) append
    their path prefix to the existing $(TOPDIR).   SDMake does not do any
    cd'ing itself and you must generally use $(TOPDIR) in your .include
    files to identify the directory path containing the include file
    currently being processed. Since you know where this file resides in
    your source hierarchy you can construct paths to things like object and
    bin directories.

    For example, this allows you to create a master SDMakefile.inc at the
    top of your project hierarchy with all the targets and sophistication
    required to control your project.  Individual SDMakefile's within the
    project hierarchy would then set up a few VAR='s and .include the master.


    The other three are created once at startup and can be overridden by a
    command-line argument or tool type:

      $(HOST_OSVER)

         Contains the exec.library version number.

      $(BUILDDATE)

         Contains the date in format: DD-MM-YY.

      $(BUILDTIME)

         Contains the time in format: hh:mm:ss.

    Additionally if "SMakefile" is (auto)selected when SDMake is started,
    the file "ENV:SDMake/SDmake-SMake.def is parsed before "SMakefile".
    This file contains by default the following assignments:

      AS = asm
      CC = sc
      CFLAGS =
      LD = slink

@endnode

@node DIRECTIVES "Directives"

			      SDMAKEFILE DIRECTIVES

    .include filename

	Include another SDMakefile.  Note that the filename is not quoted.

    .revheader filename

        Reads the -h file filename, and parse any "#define" whose name
        contains REV or VER. If they define a value, or another "#define"
        whose name matches a existing variable, create a matching variable.
        A file consisting of the following lines would create three
        variables, $(VERSION) containing '4', $(REVISION) containing '2'
        and $(OTHERVER) containing '4'.

           #define VERS		"prog v4.2"
           #define VERSION	4
           #define REVISION	2
           #define OTHERVER	VERSION
           #define SOMETHING	42
           #define SOMEVERE     SOMETHING

    .leftislabel

        Indicates that the lhs of following dependencies are made up names,
        and any existing file/dir with the same name will be ignored.

    .leftisgroup
        Same as .leftislabel but requires that lhs of the parent (or any
        ancestor) dependency is a file. This file will then be used in place
        of lhs when determining if the target is up to date like this example:

           realfile1 : sometarget1
           realfile2 : sometarget2

           .leftislabel

           sometarget1 : othertarget1

           othertarget1 : otherfile1
                echo "This will run unconditionally"

           .leftisgroup

           sometarget2 : othertarget2

           othertarget2 : otherfile2
                echo "This will only run if realfile2 is missing"
                echo "or older than otherfile2"

    .leftisfile

        Indicates that the lhs of following dependencies are real files/dirs.
        Used to switch back from .leftislabel and .leftisgroup.

    .onerror target

        Select a target to be run on failure.

    .ifdef varname

	If the variable exists

    .iffile filname

        If the file exists

    .ifeq symbol1 symbol2

        If symbol1 is equal to symbol2

    .ifgt symbol1 symbol2

        If symbol1 is greater than symbol2

    .ifin symbol symbol(s)

        If symbol is a substring of symbol(s)

    .ifos osname

        For compatibility with DMakefiles, true if supplied osname is 'AmigaOS'.

    .ifarch architecture

        For compatibility with DMakefiles, true if supplied architecture is 'm68k'.

    .else
    .endif

	These are supporting directives for conditionals.

@endnode

@node COMPATIBILITY "Compatibility"

                                COMPATIBILITY

      DMake from DICE (v2.4 and older)

        SDMake should be compatible with all DMakefiles made for DMake
        up to v2.4. Any such files that do not work should be considered,
        and preferably reported as, bugs. The following exceptions are by
        design:
        - DMake will search "dcc:bin/" if a command is not found in the
          path, while SDMake will not.
        - DMake accepts both CR+LF and LF as line endings, while SDMake
          only works with Amiga text-files (LF line endings).


      DXMake

        SDMake should should technically be compatible with most
        DMakefies made for DXMake up to v2.12, but those versions where
        never released for Amiga.


      DMake from DICE-nx (v3.20.0 and newer)

        SDMake should be compatible with all DMakefiles made for DMake
        v3.20.0 (the latest version at the time of writing). Any such
        files that do not work should be considered, and preferably
        reported as, bugs. The following exceptions are by design:
        - DMake will search "dcc:bin/" if a command is not found in the
          path, while SDMake will not.
        - DMake accepts both CR+LF and LF as line endings, while SDMake
          only works with Amiga text-files (LF line endings).

        DMakefiles created for newer DMake versions may not work
        correctly.


      SMake

        SDMake is compatible with a small subset of SMakefiles.
        The default macros AS, CC, LD, and CFLAGS are added if the
        selected makefile is named SMakefile.
        The following is not supported:
        - Fake targets.
        - Transformation rules.
        - The default macros $<, $>, $? and $@.
        - Local input files that use preface, filename, or '!'.

        Note: SMake runs the command lists of targets with a hard coded
        failat level of 1. While SDMake uses a default level of 6.
        This can be changed with the "-F" command line option or "FAILAT"
        tool type.

@endnode

@node COMMANDSHELL "Command Shell"

				COMMAND SHELL

    Under 2.0 commands that do not contain any sort of redirection are run
    with RunCommand().  If a command is an alias or there is some sort of
    redirection in the arguments it will be run with System().

    Under 1.3 everything is run in a separate shell started with Execute().

    Commands may be prefixed.  The "@" prefix tells SDMake not to echo
    the command line before it executes it.  The "-" prefix tells SDMake
    to ignore any error exit. The "#" prefix tells SDMake to skip the
    command.

    Note: Prefixing a command with "#" is not the same as starting a line
    with "#". The former creates a command that the command shell will skip
    when encountered. The latter creates a comment line the gets stripped
    out before it is passed to the command shell.

	all :
	    echo "You see this twice"
	    @echo "You see this once"

        somefile: otherfile
            #echo "SDMake will touch somefile since a command-list"
            #echo "was executed, but somefile did not change."

        someotherfile: yetafile
        #    echo "SDMake will not touch someotherfile because no"
        #    echo "command-list exists for this dependency."

    In addition to the fwrite/fappnd commands described below, SDMake also
    supports the simplest and most commonly used form of local input files
    implemented by SMake. When a command line ends with two '<' followed by
    a newline, the following lines, up until a newline followed by '<' and
    another newline, are written to a file named "temp_sdmake.tmp". And the
    two '<'s are replaced with that filename. Variables will be resolved and
    replaced with their content.

        link:
            slink WITH <<
        long list of
        link options go here

        will be written to temp_sdmake.tmp
        and the command will be changed to:
        slink WITH temp_sdmake.tmp

        <


    The command shell has a number of internal commands:

      cd path

         Change the current directory of SDMake to path.
         If no path is supplied it will cd to the initial current
         directory the SDMake was started from.


      failat level

         By default, SDMake aborts with an error if a program gives
         a return code greater than 5. This command changes this to
         limit to level until the end of the current command-list,
         or it is run again with a different level.


      fappnd arguments
      fwrite arguments

         The fwrite/fappnd command arguments has two forms:

            fwrite filename arg1 ... argN

                Write args to filename one per line.

            fwrite filename <<
            Text to write to file,
            the last character in the file
            will be the period.
            <

            	Write the following lines, up until "\n<\n" is
                encountered, to filename.

         The only difference between fwrite and fappnd is that the
         former will overwrite an existing file while the latter will
         append to it.

         Variables will be resolved and replaced with their content.


      if arguments

         The if command arguments has multiple forms:

            if defined variable

                If the variable is defined

            if exists file

                If the file exists

            if eq arg1 arg2

                If arg1 is equal to arg2

            if gt arg1 arg2

                If ar1 is greater than arg2

            if in string1 string2

                If string1 is a sub-string of string2
                (string2 can consist of multiple symbols)

            if warn

                If return code from previous AmigaDOS command was >= 5

            if error

                If return code from previous AmigaDOS command was >= 10

            if fail

                If return code from previous AmigaDOS command was >= 20


      else
      endif

         Support commands for if.


      makedir dirname

         Make the directory dirname.


      quit return

         End execution of the current command-list.
         If return is ERROR, SDMake will terminate with return code 10.
         If return is OK, SDMake will act as if the current command-list
         finished without errors.

@endnode
@node ENVIRONMENTVARIABLES "Environment Variables"

			    ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES

    2.0 local variables and 1.3/2.0 ENV: variables are fully accessible.
    Under 2.0 you can also modify local variables on the fly.
    SDMake-specific variables override 2.0 local variables override ENV:
    variables.

    Under 2.0, any command containing '<', '>', '`', or '|', or is an
    alias, will be run with System().  Thus, such commands may not be used
    to modify local variables or the local environment.	Also, such commands
    cannot be ^C'd due to the way AmigaDOS works.

@endnode

